    <paragraph>Solve Equations with Polynomial Functions</paragraph>
    <paragraph>In the following exercises, solve.</paragraph>
    <exercise number="313" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For the function, $$f(x) = x^{2} - 8x + 8,$$ ⓐ find when $$f(x) = -4$$ ⓑ Use this information to find two points that lie on the graph of the function.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>ⓐ $$x=2x=2$$ or $$x=6x=6$$ ⓑ $$(2,-4)(2,-4)$$ $$(6,-4)(6,-4)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="314" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For the function, $$f(x) = x^{2} + 11x + 20,$$ ⓐ find when $$f(x) = -8$$ ⓑ Use this information to find two points that lie on the graph of the function.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="315" has_solution="true">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For the function, $$f(x) = 8x^{2} - 18x + 5,$$ ⓐ find when $$f(x) = -4$$ ⓑ Use this information to find two points that lie on the graph of the function.</paragraph>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <paragraph>ⓐ $$x=32x=32$$ or $$x=34x=34$$ ⓑ $$(32,-4)(32,-4)$$ $$(34,-4)(34,-4)$$</paragraph>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
    <exercise number="316" has_solution="false">
      <problem>
        <paragraph>For the function, $$f(x) = 18x^{2} + 15x - 10,$$ ⓐ find when $$f(x) = 15$$ ⓑ Use this information to find two points that lie on the graph of the function.</paragraph>
      </problem>
    </exercise>
    <paragraph>In the following exercises, for each function, find: ⓐ the zeros of the function ⓑ the x-intercepts of the graph of the function ⓒ the y-intercept of the graph of the function.</paragraph>
